  The point of the "resists" message for Magicbane's cancellation
may be to complement the "cancel" message the wielder gets in the
first place.

  A wand of cancellation gives no message about what it's doing 
at all.  But the user at least knows whether the (possibly
unidentified) wand zapped a charge or not.  The attempted effect
(and possible resistance) must be identified by the user.

  Magicbane gives a message when cancellation is attempted.  
Possibly because otherwise an unspoiled wielder would have no 
idea such an effect was possible, much less happening.

  Since a message is give so the wielder knows monster 
cancellation is being attempted, it may make sense to also
let the wielder know whether it succeeds.

  An 'in-game' explanation could be that just being in physical 
contact with the monster (through Magicbane's hit) lets the 
wielder "feel" both the attempt and possible resistance.


